Limited Cutaneous Systemic Sclerosis (Mild CREST)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Cold-induced digit blanching, mild reflux, positive Centromere antibodies

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-025
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe ischemic pain in the digits requiring exclusion of critical arterial occlusion or acute rheumatological crisis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate sudden, painful, stark white discoloration of the fingers. Immediate clinical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out acute thromboembolic digit ischemia, severe vasospasm threatening tissue necrosis, or secondary infection of digital ulcers prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I went outside to get the bins, and my fingers turned completely dead white and the pain is absolutely excruciating. They feel like they're freezing from the inside out.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports primary Raynaud's phenomenon predominant, managed conservatively. Tonight, after brief exposure to cold air (10 degrees Celsius), the patient experienced a severe, prolonged vasospastic attack. Fingers turned sharply white, then cyanotic. The severe, throbbing ischemic pain has persisted for 45 minutes despite the patient returning indoors, causing extreme distress. History reveals mild reflux and previously positive Centromere antibodies. Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.
Functional Status: Independent, currently unable to use hands due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone, extreme pain limiting ability to perform basic tasks tonight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, in obvious acute pain, cradling hands against chest.
Physical Exam: Bilateral hands show cold-induced digit blanching transitioning to mild reactive hyperemia (redness) during the exam. Capillary refill is delayed (>4 seconds) in the distal phalanges but strictly recovers. Radial and ulnar pulses are strong and bounding bilaterally. Allen's test demonstrates intact palmar arches. No active digital pitting ulcers or gangrenous necrosis. Mild skin tightening noted over the fingers (sclerodactyly).
Environmental Observations: House temperature was slightly cool; advised patient to turn up heating.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient not currently taking calcium channel blockers.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Raynaud's Phenomenon attack in the setting of Limited Cutaneous Systemic Sclerosis (Mild CREST) - Status: Exacerbated, resolving
Differentials:

Acute Arterial Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, symmetrical presentation, intact strong wrist pulses, and the triphasic color change recovering with warmth.

Buerger's Disease (Thromboangiitis Obliterans) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, strictly cold-induced transient presentation rather than fixed ischemia, and negative smoking history (presumed).

Infected Digital Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of skin breakdown, purulence, or localized fluctuance on the fingertips.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (living alone, pain limiting function)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe ischemic pain in the digits secondary to profound vasospasm. Triage correctly prioritized immediate evaluation to exclude fixed acute arterial occlusion, which would require emergency surgical intervention to save the digits. Clinical bedside assessment confirmed intact macrovascular supply (strong wrist pulses) and observed the spontaneous, albeit slow, reversal of the microvascular spasm with warming. This confirmed a severe primary vasospastic flare rather than an embolic event. Immediate management with rapid rewarming techniques was essential to restore perfusion and control severe pain, entirely justifying urgent after-hours care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed patient to run warm (not hot) water over the wrists and hands. Administered fast-acting oral simple analgesia. Monitored until capillary refill normalized.
Positive Behavioral Change: Put on thick woollen socks, a sweater, and gloves immediately, and drink a large cup of hot decaffeinated tea. Raising your core body temperature rapidly, rather than just warming your hands, will command your blood vessels to open fully, completely resolving the throbbing pain within 15 minutes.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 3-5 days to commence calcium channel blockers (e.g., nifedipine) for vasospasm prevention.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if any fingertip remains completely white or blue and numb for more than two hours despite core warming, or if a finger turns black.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The blood vessels in your fingers overreact to the cold by clamping shut. Once your core body temperature warms up, the vessels will open and the pain will cease completely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if you develop an open sore on your fingertip that becomes red, swollen, and drains pus.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain was caused by temporary lack of blood flow due to a severe muscle spasm in the blood vessels. There is no permanent blockage or blood clot.
Actionable Steps: Keep your entire house warmer. You must wear gloves before reaching into the freezer or going outside in cold weather. See your GP to get medication that prevents these spasms.

